s efficient degradation of peat polyphenolic constituents by microbial aromatic oxygenases, which enhances peat carbon lability during later anaerobic microbial processing, yielding additional methanogenic precursors that stimulate methane production. The results indicate a critical role for spatiotemporal oxic-anoxic transitions in modulating wetland CH4 production, with implications for carbon-climate feedbacks and wetland management.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="5.11.AWT_16S_V4" accession="SRS5040915"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS5040915</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN12162621</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|zhanglabprinceton2019" label="Sample name">5.11.AWT_16S_V4</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>1799672</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>peat metagenome</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>551662</ID><LABEL>PRJNA551662</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>host</TAG><VALUE>not applicable</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>isolation_source</TAG><VALUE>Sphagnum peat soil</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>collection_date</TAG><VALUE>2017-06-01</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>geo_loc_name</TAG><VALUE>USA: Andover, MA, Ward Reservation</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>lat_lon</TAG><VALUE>42.38 N 71.06 W</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>Time Point</TAG><VALUE>5</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>Layer</TAG><VALUE>AWT</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>%O2 Treatment</TAG><VALUE>5</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>Sample Key order</TAG><VALUE>27</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>Metagenome or environmental</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES></SAMPLE><Pool><Member member_name="" accession="SRS5040915" sample_name="5.11.AWT_16S_V4" s